### What is the merge K sorted lists problem?

You are given an array of K linked list heads, each list sorted in ascending order. The goal is to merge all K lists into a single sorted linked list efficiently. This is the foundational problem of the K-way merge pattern, commonly used in external sorting and multi-stream aggregation.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SettlementNode {
    public String branchId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public SettlementNode next;

    public SettlementNode(String branchId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.branchId = branchId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

### How does the min-heap approach work?

Seed a `PriorityQueue` with the head of every non-empty list. Loop while the heap is not empty: poll the smallest node, attach it to the result using a dummy head pattern, and push the polled node's successor if it exists.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.SettlementNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class KListMerger {
    public static SettlementNode mergeKLists(SettlementNode[] lists) {
        PriorityQueue<SettlementNode> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> a.amount.compareTo(b.amount)
        );

        for (SettlementNode head : lists) {
            if (head != null) {
                heap.offer(head);
            }
        }

        SettlementNode dummy = new SettlementNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
        SettlementNode tail = dummy;

        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            SettlementNode smallest = heap.poll();
            tail.next = smallest;
            tail = tail.next;

            if (smallest.next != null) {
                heap.offer(smallest.next);
            }
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SettlementNode l1 = new SettlementNode("HDFC", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        l1.next = new SettlementNode("HDFC", new BigDecimal("5000"));

        SettlementNode l2 = new SettlementNode("ICICI", new BigDecimal("2000"));
        l2.next = new SettlementNode("ICICI", new BigDecimal("8000"));

        SettlementNode l3 = new SettlementNode("SBI", new BigDecimal("3000"));
        l3.next = new SettlementNode("SBI", new BigDecimal("7000"));

        SettlementNode merged = mergeKLists(new SettlementNode[]{l1, l2, l3});
        SettlementNode cur = merged;
        while (cur != null) {
            System.out.println(cur.branchId + " : " + cur.amount);
            cur = cur.next;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
HDFC : 1000
ICICI : 2000
SBI : 3000
HDFC : 5000
SBI : 7000
ICICI : 8000
```

### Why is the heap size bounded by K?

The heap holds at most one node per active list. When you poll a node, you push at most one successor. So the size never exceeds K. This is what keeps each heap operation at O(log K) rather than O(log N).

```text
Heap state during merge (K=3):
  Start:   [1000, 2000, 3000]     size = 3
  Poll 1000, push 5000: [2000, 3000, 5000]  size = 3
  Poll 2000, push 8000: [3000, 5000, 8000]  size = 3
  Poll 3000, push 7000: [5000, 7000, 8000]  size = 3
  ...
  When a list is exhausted, heap shrinks below K
```

### How is this different from naive pairwise merge?

Merging lists one pair at a time in sequence re-walks already-merged elements and degrades to O(N * K). The heap approach touches each node exactly once for insertion and extraction.

| Approach | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Heap-based K-way merge | O(N log K) | O(K) |
| Sequential pairwise merge | O(N * K) | O(1) |
| Divide-and-conquer pairwise | O(N log K) | O(1) |

```java
// NAIVE: merge one pair at a time (O(N*K) worst case)
public static SettlementNode mergeNaive(SettlementNode[] lists) {
    SettlementNode result = null;
    for (SettlementNode list : lists) {
        result = mergeTwoLists(result, list); // re-walks result each time
    }
    return result;
}
```

### What is the divide-and-conquer alternative?

Instead of a heap, you can pair adjacent lists and merge them, then pair the results and merge again, repeating for log K rounds. This also achieves O(N log K) time but uses O(1) extra space because it only relinks nodes.

```java
public static SettlementNode mergeKDivideConquer(SettlementNode[] lists) {
    if (lists == null || lists.length == 0) return null;
    int interval = 1;
    while (interval < lists.length) {
        for (int i = 0; i + interval < lists.length; i += interval * 2) {
            lists[i] = mergeTwoLists(lists[i], lists[i + interval]);
        }
        interval *= 2;
    }
    return lists[0];
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty arrays, arrays with all null heads, and arrays with a single list are the key edge cases.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.SettlementNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class KMergeEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Empty array
        System.out.println("Empty: " + KListMerger.mergeKLists(new SettlementNode[]{}));

        // All nulls
        System.out.println("All null: " + KListMerger.mergeKLists(
            new SettlementNode[]{null, null}));

        // Single list
        SettlementNode single = new SettlementNode("SBI", new BigDecimal("500"));
        SettlementNode result = KListMerger.mergeKLists(new SettlementNode[]{single});
        System.out.println("Single: " + result.branchId + " " + result.amount);
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: null
All null: null
Single: SBI 500
```

### Why use compareTo for BigDecimal in the comparator?

Never compare money with `equals` or subtraction. `BigDecimal.equals` checks scale, so `5000` and `5000.00` are not equal. `compareTo` checks numeric value only, which is what you want for ordering.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// WRONG: subtraction-based comparator can overflow with extreme values
// and does not handle BigDecimal correctly
(a, b) -> a.amount.subtract(b.amount).intValue() // never do this

// CORRECT: compareTo handles all BigDecimal edge cases
(a, b) -> a.amount.compareTo(b.amount)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The K-way merge pattern appears in external sorting (merging K sorted files), database query execution (merging sorted index scans), and stream processing. The heap-based version streams naturally as data arrives, which fits a feed-based banking system where branch settlement data arrives incrementally.

In production at National Bank of India, we use this to aggregate settlement feeds from multiple regional branches, each sending instructions already sorted by amount. The central engine maintains a size-K min-heap and drains it into one globally sorted stream for the ledger writer.

```java
// In a streaming context, you can lazily pull from each list
// and the heap ensures globally sorted output at all times
while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    SettlementNode next = heap.poll();
    ledgerWriter.write(next.branchId, next.amount);
    if (next.next != null) heap.offer(next.next);
}
```
